        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a1fab0ef-ec90-5870-88f9-d8b3e0c12170",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Appointment in Tokyo, USS Missouri (BB-63)\n- Organizations: Army Pictorial Service, Signal Corps, Army Air Forces, United States Navy, Warner Bros., War Activities Committee\n- People: General Douglas MacArthur\n- Events: surrender of Japan, signing of the instrument of surrender, World War II\n\nFacts:\n- In early 1942, Appointment in Tokyo follows General Douglas MacArthur and his men from their exile from the Philippines.\n- On September 1, 1945, the instrument of surrender of Japan was signed on the USS Missouri.\n- Appointment in Tokyo is a 1945 documentary produced by the Army Pictorial Service, Signal Corps, with the cooperation of the Army Air Forces and the United States Navy.\n- Appointment in Tokyo was released by Warner Bros. for the War Activities Committee shortly after the surrender of Japan.\n- Appointment in Tokyo mainly follows General Douglas MacArthur and his men through the signing of the instrument of surrender on the USS Missouri.\n- The USS Missouri was the last battleship commissioned by the United States.\n- The USS Missouri is best remembered as the site of the surrender of the Empire of Japan that ended World War II.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "96b91738-9404-5423-bc31-3480a240265d",
        "text": "Page: Appointment in Tokyo. Sentence: Appointment in Tokyo is a 1945 documentary released Produced by the Army Pictorial Service, Signal Corps, with the cooperation of the Army Air Forces and the United States Navy, and released by Warner Bros. for the War Activities Committee shortly after the surrender of Japan.\nPage: Appointment in Tokyo. Sentence: It mainly follows General Douglas MacArthur and his men from their exile from the Philippines in early 1942, through the signing of the instrument of surrender on the USS \"Missouri\" on September 1, 1945.\nPage: USS Missouri (BB-63). Sentence: \"Missouri\" was the last battleship commissioned by the United States and is best remembered as the site of the surrender of the Empire of Japan which ended World War II.",
